{"answer":"According to ADR-050 (status "in progress", dated 2026-10-09), Lore's metrics are collected through Prometheus and viewed in Grafana.

**Collection**
- Every Lore service serves OpenTelemetry metrics as Prometheus text on a `metrics` port. The default is 9464 and `LORE_METRICS_PORT` overrides it. The export uses `@opentelemetry/exporter-prometheus`.
- lore-api hands the Prometheus reader to its NodeSDK, next to the Cloud Trace exporter, which still handles traces. The stations service and the lore-mcp gateway each register their own MeterProvider.
- The Cloud Monitoring metric exporter has been removed, so Cloud Monitoring no longer holds any Lore metric.
- All Lore instruments live in `libs/shared/src/outbound/otel/metrics.ts`. They are resolved lazily against the meter provider registered at record time, and each thing is recorded at exactly one site.
- The metrics cover:
  - tool calls and HTTP requests;
  - station runs and durations;
  - bus deliveries and queue depth;
  - floor line starts;
  - GitHub webhook events;
  - model calls, tokens and cost;
  - billed spend (the `lore.billed.cost_usd` gauge).
- The floor and the ai-agent-subsystem controller keep their own hand-written `/metrics` endpoints, and Prometheus scrapes them as they are.

**Scraping and viewing**
- Terraform installs kube-prometheus-stack and Grafana in a `monitoring` namespace, behind the `enable_monitoring` flag. PodMonitors and ServiceMonitors cover every service.
- Grafana is served at grafana.gcp.re-cinq.com, behind oauth2-proxy with the same Google client as Headlamp. It also reads GKE pod metrics through a Cloud Monitoring datasource.
- Five dashboards were populated on first login. They name no assembly line: the per-line dashboard uses a `line` variable read from the metric labels, so a new line appears by itself.
- The web UI links to Grafana only when terraform provides `GRAFANA_URL`.","sources":["adrs/ADR-050-prometheus-grafana-metrics.md"]}

